## Source progress — 2026-11-10: initial M3 key-install integrity

The initial four-way M3 key-install path now verifies that a native backend
has not rewritten the borrowed pairwise TK/PTK, GTK or authenticated GTK
receive sequence counter while returning success. A post-callback mismatch
fails the handshake, invokes installed-key rollback and does not publish M4
or authorize the controlled port. The check complements the existing
post-install Group1 rekey integrity checks; both preserve callback epoch
revocation semantics and erase temporary key snapshots on all paths.

Three synthetic callback-mutation scenarios reproduce an erroneous successful
M4 with the preceding implementation (exit 26 in the old-source fixture),
and pass with the new checks under C++14 and C++17 ASan+UBSan. This is an
offline backend-contract regression, not proof of a physical keycache,
constant-time crypto, real scan/connect, native supplicant integration or
hardware operation. The production and WPA2 integration TODOs remain open.
